  +            /* **The three readings do not ask the same kind of question, and
  +             * the window must not point the same way for all of them.**
  +             *
  +             * INFEASIBLE and REDUNDANT each prove an INEQUALITY — that no
  +             * point of the boxes can satisfy the row, or that every point
  +             * does. A proof of an inequality wants the activity end widened
  +             * OUTWARD, so the claim survives the rounding in the sum. That is
  +             * Fourer & Gay's directed rounding (1994, "Directed Roundings"),
  +             * and it is reached here without touching the FP environment:
  +             * `fesetround` needs a pragma GCC does not fully implement and
  +             * puts ambient state where D8 forbids it, while `ps_row_tol`
  +             * already supplies exactly the quantity the widening needs.
  +             *
  +             * FORCING asks something else. It detects EQUALITY — the range's
  +             * end has reached the row's own bound, so every column is pinned.
  +             * Widening outward does not make that test safer; it destroys it.
  +             * `x0 + x1 <= 0` with both columns in [0, 10] is the canonical
  +             * case, its minimum activity is exactly 0 and its upper bound is
  +             * exactly 0, and one ulp of outward widening declines it. That is
  +             * `tests/test_presolve.c`'s own `make_forcing_row_model`, and it
  +             * is what a first version of this change broke.
  +             *
  +             * So: outward for the two proofs, inward for the detection.
  +             *
  +             * The INFEASIBLE tests come out bit-identical either way, since
  +             * `min_act - err > ru` is `min_act > ru + err`. **REDUNDANT is
  +             * where this changes behaviour**, and it changes it from unsound
  +             * to sound: the old form dropped a row whose minimum activity was
  +             * within `err` BELOW `rl`, which is a row that can still bind.
  +             * Fourer & Gay report exactly that failure — AMPL "discard[ing]
  +             * constraints that kept the problem from being unbounded" on
  +             * netlib's `maros`, one of D97's own four instances.
  +             *
  +             * The frozen-row reading at the bottom of this file is left alone
  +             * deliberately: it scales by `ps_bound_scale` rather than traffic
  +             * (docs/tolerances.md says why) and asks only the INFEASIBLE
  +             * question, which does not move.
  +             *
  +             * Named `err` and not `w`: `w` is the work counter in this scope,
  +             * and shadowing it compiles as a silent type error at the next
  +             * jm_work_add. -Werror caught it; the name is what stops it. */
